Z||000008||Slammed Fiero||09-19-2001||12:21 PM||||No offense to someone engineering their own setup. But when it comes to something that has coolant passing through it I would want a warrantied piece of work like thrasher offers that is milled out of billet, and extensively tested by an outfit that will be around tomorrow and has been around for a while.<P>Damm those guys have fast cars on the clubGP forum.<P>JM ||||reg||1
Z||000009||MinnGreenGT||09-19-2001||12:24 PM||||When you're trying to pull as much power from a V6 as that, especially when dealing with that high of PSi... going the cheap route is not usually wise.  Like I said above- the concept is certainly cool- and combining it with an Intercooler would be even better!  <P>This is how I understand the physics of it:<BR>Air temp is altered by the air molecules slowing down when coming into contact with a cold material (faster-hotter, slower-colder).  An intercooler has a large amount of surface area for the air to pass through and be cooled.  The Supercharger itself doesn't have as much surface area- therefore even if it is colder, it won't alter the air temperature as much as an intercooler.  Think of your radiator... it has all the little fins to create more surface area- this allows the incoming air to cool a larger amount of area, effectively reducing the coolant temp (by energy/themal transfer).  If your radiator didn't have those fins- and you just cooled the frame of the radiator, your coolant temperature wouldn't be as low...<P>...like I said- at least that's how I understand it.||||reg||1
Z||000010||pontiacs4me||09-19-2001||03:53 PM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by MinnGreenGT:<BR>[B]This place makes pullies down to the 3" AND an intercooler that still fits under the hood (barely).  The guy at Dells with the Aldino was running the 3" pulley and intercooler- nice.  Why recreate the wheel?<BR><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P> <P>First off it doesnt fit under the ram air hoods that everyone is running these days, they have to cut a notch out of the supports for one of the vaccum modules on the blower.  <BR>Why recreate the wheel?  When the wheel was invented it was wooden and a piece of crap, look now adays, we have 23" wheels that are awesome (well some of them), theres always room for refinement.  <BR>A couple more pieces of info.  Thrashers IC is close to $2300, because the design of it sucks.  the  intercooler core becomes so much of an air restriction, that you have to send them your blower and intake manifold for modification.  They open up the mounting surface of the blower, and open up the bottom of the blower, so the thing doesnt actually lose power.  On Toms supercooler you dont have to modify anything.  Its practically a bolt in job. <P>              Quote:  No offense to someone engineering their own setup. But when it comes to something that has coolant passing through it I would want a warrantied piece of work like thrasher offers<BR>                that is milled out of billet, and extensively tested by an outfit that will be around tomorrow and has been around for a while.<BR> <BR> Tom is going in on this deal with MSP.  MotorSports Performance. It will be warranteed, guaranteed and all that.<BR>BTW the final product will be CNC phenolic.  <BR>This product will be seeing a dyno very soon, on a totally stock GTP, so we will see some numbers soon.<P><BR>                Quote: When you're trying to pull as much power from a V6 as that, especially when dealing with that high of PSi... going the cheap route is not usually wise.<P>So what should we do raise the price?  Thrasher is sh!tting their pants right now.  We have a product that is equally effective as theirs, which is 30% of the price and requires no intake/blower modification.  We will just see.  <BR>Tom also picked up .3 on his quarter mile time, besides the fact that the engine used to ping under hard acceleration with the 3.4 pulley and 91 octane on a hot day.  Now he has a 3" pulley, 91 octane, its cooling off outside now, but not a bit of ping, or KR.<p>[This message has been edited by pontiacs4me (edited 09-19-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000011||MinnGreenGT||09-19-2001||04:49 PM||||First off, this is not meant to be a flame war... relax a little.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by pontiacs4me:<BR><B>   <P>First off it doesnt fit under the ram air hoods that everyone is running these days...<P>...because the design of it sucks.  the  intercooler core becomes so much of an air restriction, that you have to send them your blower and intake manifold for modification.  They open up the mounting surface of the blower, and open up the bottom of the blower, so the thing doesnt actually lose power... <P>We have a product that is equally effective as theirs, which is 30% of the price and requires no intake/blower modification.  We will just see...<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>Note that you have posted this on a FIERO forum... I don't seem to recall any Fieros with "Ram-Air" hoods- point good for GPs, but meaningless to Fieros.<P>As to the blower mods (ref to air restriction)... did you catch the physics info on surface area and cooling properties?<P>We?  Didn't realize that you were quite that deeply involved in the engineering of the project!  I've never said that the concept was flawed or bad or anything to that extent.  If I had the cash to install my Series II 3800 SC, I'd do it... then I'd save up a bit more cash and get your Blower-cooler, then a hunk more cash and an intercooler! And run as small of a pulley as possible!<P>Like I said... no flames inteded here- just my understanding of the physics involved and my opinion relating to it.||||reg||14
